CR=ref('NIH: Cryptococcosis, Adult and Adolescent OIs','https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/guidelines/hiv-clinical-guidelines-adult-and-adolescent-opportunistic-infections/cryptococcosis')
CG=ref('ECMM/ISHAM/ASM Global Guideline for Cryptococcosis, 2024','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11526416/')
CM=ref('NIH: Cytomegalovirus Disease','https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/guidelines/hiv-clinical-guidelines-adult-and-adolescent-opportunistic-infections/cytomegalovirus')
MC=ref('NIH: Disseminated Mycobacterium avium Complex','https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/guidelines/hiv-clinical-guidelines-adult-and-adolescent-opportunistic-infections/disseminated')
q('An untreated patient with advanced HIV has two weeks of headache and minimal neck stiffness. Which diagnostic approach is most appropriate for suspected cryptococcal meningitis?', 'Assess CSF cryptococcal antigen and fungal culture, measuring opening pressure during an appropriate lumbar puncture',[
 ('Exclude meningitis because neck stiffness is mild','Advanced immunodeficiency can blunt meningeal signs.'),('Use serum cryptococcal antigen alone to determine that CNS disease is absent','Positive antigen can require CNS evaluation; serum testing does not measure CSF pressure.'),('Rely on a normal peripheral leukocyte count to rule it out','The blood count does not exclude CNS infection.')],
 'A subacute meningitic syndrome can be clinically understated in advanced HIV', '**Cryptococcal antigen is a highly useful diagnostic test.**',['Culture evaluates viable organisms and subsequent sterilization.','Opening pressure is essential because raised ICP is treatable and dangerous.'], '**Assess LP safety and obtain appropriate neuroimaging when clinically indicated.**',['Absence of dramatic CSF inflammation does not exclude disease.','Begin effective antifungal treatment promptly when the diagnosis is established or strongly suspected.'],[['Antigen','Diagnostic evidence'],['Culture','Viable organism assessment'],['Opening pressure','Identifies a major management problem']],[CR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('In a resource-available setting, which induction regimen is preferred for HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis?', 'Liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine',[
 ('Low-dose fluconazole alone as equivalent induction','It is not the preferred fungicidal induction strategy.'),('An echinocandin alone','Echinocandins do not provide effective cryptococcal treatment.'),('TMP-SMX alone','It treats selected other opportunistic infections, not cryptococcal meningitis.')],
 'High fungal burden requires an effective induction phase', '**Amphotericin-based combination induction is followed by consolidation and maintenance.**',['Renal function, potassium, magnesium and blood counts require monitoring.','Flucytosine dosing and toxicity monitoring are particularly important with renal impairment.'], '**Use the regimen appropriate to the resource setting and guideline.**',['Validated single-dose liposomal amphotericin combination strategies exist in resource-limited settings.','Do not confuse maintenance fluconazole with induction therapy.'],[['Induction','Liposomal amphotericin plus flucytosine'],['Consolidation','Fluconazole-based phase'],['Maintenance','Continue until appropriate recovery criteria']],[CR,CG])
q('A patient with cryptococcal meningitis has severe headache and CSF opening pressure 36 cm H2O. Which intervention is central?', 'Therapeutic CSF drainage with repeated lumbar punctures as indicated, alongside antifungal therapy',[
 ('Mannitol as the routine definitive pressure treatment','It is not the recommended routine approach for cryptococcal ICP.'),('Acetazolamide as the standard substitute for CSF drainage','It is not routinely recommended and can cause harm.'),('High-dose corticosteroids for every pressure elevation','Routine steroids are not the recommended management.')],
 'Raised intracranial pressure is a distinct treatable cause of morbidity', '**Measure pressure and document the response to drainage.**',['Repeated therapeutic LPs may be necessary.','Persistent problems can require specialist CSF diversion.'], '**Treat pressure and infection together.**',['Antifungals alone do not immediately resolve the pressure problem.','Monitor vision, mental status and recurrent symptoms.'],[['Problem','Raised CSF pressure'],['Primary measure','Therapeutic drainage'],['Persistent elevation','Repeat assessment and possible diversion']],[CR,CG],style='clinical_scenario')
q('When is ART generally started in a patient beginning treatment for HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis?', 'Usually deferred for about four to six weeks after antifungal treatment begins',[
 ('Always on the same day as antifungal induction','Very early ART can increase dangerous CNS inflammatory complications.'),('Deferred permanently because ART causes IRIS','ART remains essential after the appropriate delay.'),('Only after the CD4 count spontaneously normalizes without ART','That is not a realistic or recommended strategy.')],
 'CNS infection is an important exception to routine immediate ART', '**Delayed initiation reduces the risk of harmful cryptococcal CNS IRIS.**',['Coordinate timing with clinical progress and antifungal response.','Manage ICP throughout the early course.'], '**Plan ART rather than losing the patient to follow-up.**',['Check interactions and adherence readiness.','Use specific guidance for unusual circumstances such as pregnancy.'],[['General HIV care','Prompt ART'],['CNS cryptococcosis','Usually delay 4–6 weeks'],['During delay','Effective antifungals and ICP management']],[CR,CG])
q('After appropriate cryptococcal treatment, serum antigen remains positive although the patient improves and CSF culture is sterile. What is the best interpretation?', 'Persistent antigen alone does not establish microbiological treatment failure',[
 ('Any antigen positivity proves viable persistent fungi','Antigen can persist after microbiological response.'),('Increase treatment indefinitely until every antigen test is negative','This can cause unnecessary toxicity.'),('Stop all antifungal phases immediately because symptoms improved','Consolidation and maintenance still follow the treatment plan.')],
 'Antigen detection and viable-organism recovery are different endpoints', '**Clinical course and culture are important in assessing relapse or failure.**',['Recurrent symptoms also require pressure and IRIS assessment.','Do not interpret antigen titers as the sole response measure.'], '**Investigate actual deterioration systematically.**',['Check adherence, drug exposure and culture when indicated.','Continue the recommended phase-based regimen.'],[['Antigen','May persist'],['Culture','Assesses viable organism'],['Deterioration','Evaluate relapse, pressure and IRIS']],[CR,CG])
q('A person with HIV and CD4 25 develops painless floaters and visual-field loss. What is the best immediate next step?', 'Urgent dilated ophthalmological examination for possible CMV retinitis',[
 ('Wait for a blood CMV PCR before examining the retina','Blood testing has limited diagnostic utility and should not delay sight-saving assessment.'),('Treat as migraine without an eye examination','The immune status and new symptoms require urgent retinal evaluation.'),('Exclude CMV because the eye is not painful','CMV retinitis can be painless.')],
 'New visual symptoms in advanced HIV require urgent examination', '**CMV retinitis is often diagnosed from characteristic retinal findings.**',['Blood PCR positivity does not itself establish retinitis.','A negative blood result does not reliably exclude end-organ disease.'], '**Involve an ophthalmologist promptly.**',['Sight-threatening location affects the treatment plan.','Systemic anti-CMV therapy and ART coordination are important.'],[['Symptoms','Floaters or field loss'],['Diagnosis','Dilated retinal examination'],['Blood PCR','Cannot substitute for eye assessment']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which systemic drug is commonly used for induction of CMV retinitis when oral absorption and the clinical situation permit?', 'Valganciclovir',[
 ('Acyclovir at the usual genital-herpes dose','It is inadequate treatment for CMV retinitis.'),('Fluconazole','It is an antifungal, not an anti-CMV drug.'),('Azithromycin','It is not CMV retinitis therapy.')],
 'CMV end-organ disease requires an active anti-CMV agent', '**Oral valganciclovir or IV ganciclovir are standard systemic options.**',['Central sight-threatening disease can need additional intravitreal treatment.','Route depends on severity and absorption.'], '**Monitor blood counts and renal function.**',['Adjust dosing for kidney function.','Ophthalmological response determines induction completion and later maintenance planning.'],[['Systemic options','Valganciclovir or IV ganciclovir'],['Sight-threatening lesion','May require local adjunct'],['Safety','Marrow and renal monitoring']],[CM])
q('A patient with advanced HIV has diarrhea and a positive blood CMV PCR. What establishes CMV colitis most convincingly?', 'Compatible endoscopic lesions with tissue evidence of CMV disease',[
 ('The positive blood PCR alone','Viremia does not by itself establish colonic end-organ disease.'),('A low CD4 count alone','It indicates risk, not the specific cause of diarrhea.'),('Any positive stool bacterial culture as proof of CMV colitis','It detects a different process and may indicate a competing diagnosis.')],
 'End-organ attribution requires evidence at the diseased site', '**CMV colitis assessment commonly requires endoscopy and biopsy.**',['Histology and immunostaining can demonstrate tissue involvement.','Several other infections and noninfectious causes produce diarrhea in advanced HIV.'], '**Interpret tissue findings with the clinical syndrome.**',['Do not treat isolated viral detection as equivalent to invasive disease.','Assess hydration, severity and alternative causes while arranging diagnosis.'],[['Blood CMV','Risk or replication evidence'],['Colitis','Site-specific disease'],['Confirmation','Compatible tissue involvement']],[CM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with HIV, CD4 15, fever, weight loss, anemia and elevated alkaline phosphatase has no clear focal source. Which infection deserves particular consideration?', 'Disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex',[
 ('Isolated uncomplicated mucosal candidiasis','It does not usually explain this disseminated systemic pattern.'),('Latent TB infection alone','Latent infection should not cause this syndrome.'),('Asymptomatic Toxoplasma IgG positivity','Prior exposure alone does not explain the illness.')],
 'Profound immunodeficiency permits disseminated mycobacterial disease', '**MAC can involve blood, marrow, liver and other tissues.**',['The presentation can include constitutional symptoms, cytopenias and organ abnormalities.','Other disseminated infections and lymphoma remain important alternatives.'], '**Obtain appropriate mycobacterial cultures and site-directed assessment.**',['A routine bacterial blood culture is not the complete diagnostic strategy.','Exclude active disease before prescribing prophylactic monotherapy.'],[['Risk','Very low CD4'],['Syndrome','Disseminated systemic illness'],['Diagnosis','Appropriate culture and tissue assessment']],[MC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A newly diagnosed person with HIV has CD4 35 and immediately starts a fully suppressive ART regimen. What is the usual MAC primary-prophylaxis recommendation?', 'Routine MAC primary prophylaxis is not recommended solely because of the low count when effective ART is immediately initiated',[
 ('Azithromycin prophylaxis is mandatory for everyone below 50 regardless of ART','Current guidance includes the ART context.'),('Give single-drug prophylaxis despite symptoms suggesting active disseminated MAC','Active disease must first be assessed to avoid inadequate monotherapy.'),('Withhold ART until MAC prophylaxis has been taken for six months','This is not recommended.')],
 'Effective ART changes opportunistic-infection prevention needs', '**MAC prophylaxis is considered below CD4 50 when not receiving effective suppressive ART or when viremia persists.**',['Immediate effective ART removes the routine primary-prophylaxis indication.','Assess compatible symptoms for active disease.'], '**Distinguish prevention from treatment.**',['Confirmed disseminated MAC requires combination therapy.','Review immune and virological response over time.'],[['Immediate effective ART','No routine primary MAC prophylaxis'],['CD4 <50 without suppression','Assess prophylaxis indication'],['Symptoms','Exclude active disease']],[MC])
q('Which regimen principle is central to treatment of disseminated MAC in HIV?', 'Use at least a macrolide plus ethambutol, with additional agents in selected severe or high-risk cases',[
 ('Macrolide monotherapy for established disseminated disease','This promotes resistance and is inadequate.'),('Isoniazid monotherapy as if treating latent TB','It does not constitute appropriate MAC treatment.'),('Fluconazole alone','It does not treat mycobacteria.')],
 'Combination therapy limits resistance and improves disease control', '**Azithromycin or clarithromycin is paired with ethambutol.**',['Additional therapy, such as rifabutin, is considered in selected circumstances.','Susceptibility and drug interactions influence the plan.'], '**Coordinate effective ART and adequate treatment duration.**',['Monitor clinical and microbiological response.','Ethambutol eye toxicity and rifamycin interactions require attention.'],[['Core','Macrolide plus ethambutol'],['Selected cases','Additional active agent'],['HIV care','Effective ART and interaction review']],[MC])

